---
description: language-agnostic code craft (DRY, comments, early returns, strict typing at boundaries)
alwaysApply: false
---
Apply these craft rules for frontend and backend work in any language. Pair with stack-specific architecture rules.

### Craft

- Write concise, technical code. Prefer iteration and modularization over duplication.
- Add comments only for anything that is not straightforward or self-explanatory.
- Do not add comments or documentation that reads the same as the code it describes.
- Prefer functions/components that do one job — better two focused units than one with conditionals that serve different purposes.
- Prefer early returns and guard clauses for error/precondition handling; place the happy path last; avoid unnecessary else.
- Prefer clear, small functions; avoid deep nesting.

### Composition

- Multi-step workflows (feature service methods, usecases, workers, containers) **may** exist as orchestrators: they sequence steps and handle workflow-level errors only.
- Each step with its own rule, IO, or transform belongs in a **single-job function** (same package/module is fine). Prefer extract-until-one-job over a long procedural body.
- Recurse: if a step still does multiple jobs, split again until leaves are single-purpose.
- Do not invent speculative layer folders for this — flat helpers next to the orchestrator are enough until real reuse appears (see Shared code).
- Unit tests target those leaves; orchestrator behavior is covered by higher-level tests (see `testing.mdc`).

### Shared code

- Extract shared code only after real reuse across call sites — do not pre-create shared packages for speculative reuse.
- When extracting, keep the shared portion free of call-site-specific conditionals; handle differences at each call site.

### Types and contracts

- Prefer explicit types at API and module boundaries (request/response DTOs, public functions, shared domain models). Do not rely on implicit/untyped payloads when the language supports typing.
- Validate and parse untrusted or external input at the boundary into typed domain shapes; do not leak raw untyped data deep into the app.
- Prefer the language’s typed constructs over escape hatches that erase type information. Language-specific escape hatches and checkers live in go-core, rust-core, frontend-core, and stack rules.
- Prefer generated or schema-backed types for SQL, protobuf, and OpenAPI/JSON contracts when those tools are in use — do not hand-edit generated type output.

### Identifiers

- Prefer UUID v7 (uuid7) for new persistent entity identifiers (DB primary keys and public IDs) unless an existing contract still uses another scheme (migrate toward uuid7 for new work).
